INT. OCEAN LINER - WIRELESS ROOM - NIGHT (1912)

A cramped cabin lined with brass dials and copper coils. A single lamp swings on a chain. The sound of the sea is a low roar beyond the bulkhead.

OTTOLINE FARRINGTON-ADE (20s, wireless operator, jacket too large, headphones clamped over her ears) taps a telegraph key. She is thin, pale, her fingers raw from hours of work.

PERCIVAL SUTTON-KAGAN (30s, junior officer, his collar loosened) sits beside her, a stack of messages on his knee.

CAPTAIN WEXFORD (60s, white beard, frayed cap) stands in the doorway, filling it.

She keys a final burst and listens. Static. A long hiss.

PERCIVAL
Anything?

OTTOLINE
Not yet.

CAPTAIN WEXFORD
Keep sending.

She nods and taps again. SOS. SOS. The key clicks. Percival watches her hand.

He glances at the captain. The captain says nothing.

A new sound in the headphones. A thin, far voice. Ottoline stiffens.

OTTOLINE
(whisper)
Someone's answering.

She presses the headset tighter. Her eyes widen.

CARPATHIA OPERATOR (V.O.)
(over wireless, faint)
Titanic, this is Carpathia. We are coming. Hold on.

Percival gasps. Captain Wexford closes his eyes. Ottoline bursts into tears and laughs at the same time.

OTTOLINE
Four hours!

PERCIVAL
Four hours!

CAPTAIN WEXFORD
Tell them to hurry.

Ottoline taps the reply. Her fingers steady. When she finishes, she leans back and shuts her eyes.

The lamp stops swinging. For the first time that night, the room is still.
